A C program terminates when it finishes executing. If a program reaches the end of its main function, it has finished executing and will terminate.

If a program does not reach the end of its main function, it will not terminate. Instead, the program will stop executing before reaching the end of the main function. This is usually because of an error, such as dividing by zero.

To intentionally stop a program from executing, you can use the exit function. The exit function causes the program to terminate immediately.

Here is an example of using the exit function to terminate a program:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  printf("Hello, world!\n");
  exit(0);
}
```
The program above will print "Hello, world!" to the console, and then terminate.
